WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
INTRO:
The job of being a hedge fund&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:07.760
manager is often met with a lot of controversy. It&nbsp;
seems like their jobs are not only boring but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:12.720
lack meaning. They generally work 80 to 100&nbsp;
hours per week for decades just to make their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.080
clients richer. And then, there’s also the whole&nbsp;
manipulation side of things that many despise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:22.640
Even some of the highest profile figures in the&nbsp;
world like Elon Musk have knocked on the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:27.280
being a fund manager. But despite the criticism,&nbsp;
hedge fund managers are some of the highest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.920
paid individuals in the world, often earning&nbsp;
hundreds of millions if not billions per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:36.880
And the man who topped the list in 2020 was a&nbsp;
man named Izzy Englander who pulled in a whopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.880
$3.8 billion. His flagship fund was up 26% last&nbsp;
year which was their strongest performance in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.440
20 years. Meanwhile, second place went&nbsp;
to the famous Jim Simons who earned over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.160
a billion dollars less at $2.6 billion. So,&nbsp;
here’s how Izzy Englander beat out even the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.840
most iconic managers in the world and became&nbsp;
the world’s highest paid hedge fund manager.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.160
EARLY LIFE:&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.800
Taking a look back, Izzy was born in 1948&nbsp;
in Brooklyn to a Jewish migrant family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:12.800
His parents were originally from Poland, but they&nbsp;
moved to the US in 1947 after they were extradited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.360
to a Soviet labor camp following World War 2.&nbsp;
You could say that his parents left everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.600
behind to come to the US, but the sad truth&nbsp;
was that there was nothing left for them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.480
Poland in the first place. All of Izzy’s father’s&nbsp;
family and much of his mother’s family were killed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.640
during the Holocaust. Despite all the pain and&nbsp;
discrimination the family had gone through because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:35.200
of their religion, the family remained devout Jews&nbsp;
and that’s still the case with Izzy to this day.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.080
Anyway, Izzy developed a strong interest in&nbsp;
the stock market from a rather young age.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.720
By the time he was in high school, he was already&nbsp;
trading stocks in his free time. Izzy would go on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:50.480
to attend New York University where he majored in&nbsp;
finance, and after graduation in 1970, he found a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:55.520
job at the Wall Street firm Kaufmann, Alsberg,&nbsp;
&amp; Co. His job at Kaufmann consisted of trading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:00.640
convertible safeties and options which were new&nbsp;
financial instruments at the time. As many y’all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.520
probably know, options are extremely volatile&nbsp;
and can move hundreds of percent in a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:10.800
so this job really taught Izzy how to be extremely&nbsp;
disciplined and manage risk. In the meantime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.960
Izzy enrolled in NYU’s MBA program&nbsp;
and attended classes in the evenings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.880
but he never ended up completing the program&nbsp;
as he would get distracted by an opportunity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.480
You see, around the same time, the American&nbsp;
Stock Exchange also began to list options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.280
and Izzy would go ahead and buy a seat on the&nbsp;
exchange. This allowed Izzy to create his own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:35.840
floor brokerage house called IA Englander &amp; Co,&nbsp;
and that brings us into Izzy’s first business.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.920
SERIAL ENTREPRENEUR:&nbsp;

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.200
IA Englander &amp; Co was just a simple brokerage&nbsp;
that allowed customers to buy and sell stocks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.080
bonds. While it was nothing groundbreaking, the&nbsp;
company was profitable and it’s actually still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.950
around to this day. Izzy still even holds a stake&nbsp;
in the company, but he’s not involved in its day&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:57.920
to day operations. Anyway, after running this&nbsp;
company for several years, Izzy would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:03.600
an investment firm called Jamie Securities &amp;&nbsp;
Co with a man named John Mulheren Jr in 1985.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:08.960
But, this business wasn’t nearly as smooth as the&nbsp;
first. In the beginning, it looked like things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:13.840
were going great though as the Belzberg family&nbsp;
would invest $75 million into the company. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.960
problem though was that John had a dark past that&nbsp;
would come to haunt him. Before opening a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:24.400
with Izzy, John used to work for a man named&nbsp;
Ivan Boesky who would end up being convicted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.840
insider trading. Ivan would testify against&nbsp;
John in return for a reduced sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.720
and John would end up being convicted for aiding&nbsp;
in insider trading. Izzy wasn’t associated with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.880
this incident, but given all of the bad&nbsp;
press John had garnered for the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:49.760
Izzy had no choice but to shut it down. Izzy&nbsp;
didn’t waste any time looking back though. By&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.640
this point, Izzy had become quite wealthy thanks&nbsp;
to his first two businesses and he would use this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:59.760
money to start up Millennium Management with&nbsp;
Ronald Shear in 1989. Izzy put in $5 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.960
of his own money and the Belzberg family would&nbsp;
put in another $2 million. Once again though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.960
the company would get off to a rough start and&nbsp;
Ronald would leave the company just one year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.240
later in 1990. But, Izzy wasn’t willing to start&nbsp;
over again, so he would continue nonetheless.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.080
MILLENNIUM STRATEGIES:&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.120
In terms of growing millennium&nbsp;
management and generating profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:25.840
Izzy leveraged three key strategies with the&nbsp;
first being convertible arbitrage. A convertible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.720
security is usually a corporate bond or preferred&nbsp;
stock of a given company that came to be converted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.520
into regular stock. With convertible arbitrage,&nbsp;
fund managers are looking for discrepancies in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.720
price between convertible securities and common&nbsp;
stock. For example, maybe Tesla preferred stock is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:47.040
trading at $1000 while the common stock is trading&nbsp;
at $1005. The idea is that the gap will close and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.920
that both securities will eventually trade for the&nbsp;
same price. In such a scenario, the fund manager&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.000
will buy the preferred stock at $1000 and short&nbsp;
the common stock at $1005. If you look closely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.800
you’ll notice that this leaves the manager with&nbsp;
no directional bias. It doesn’t matter if Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:08.480
doubles tomorrow or if it crashes 80%. As long as&nbsp;
the discrepancy between the preferred and common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.240
stock remains $5, the manager loses no money. By&nbsp;
eliminating directional bias, managers are able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:19.440
heavily reduce risk, but that doesn’t mean that&nbsp;
there’s no risk. If the discrepancy between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.560
two securities grows, you start to lose money fast&nbsp;
as you’re bleeding money from both sides. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:31.600
extremely rare, but it has happened before. One&nbsp;
example of this was with GM stock in 2005. At the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.120
time, many hedge funds had long positions in GM&nbsp;
convertible bonds and short positions in GM stock.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:41.440
But then suddenly, GM’s credit was downgraded&nbsp;
which caused their convertible bonds to crash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:46.400
in value. You would think that the stock would&nbsp;
also follow suit, but a billionaire investor was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:52.080
actively buying the dip which propped up the stock&nbsp;
price and made the disparity explode. So clearly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.280
things can go bad, but in most cases, convertible&nbsp;
arbitrage is an easy way to capitalize on pricing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:02.720
discrepancies with low risk. Moving onto Izzy's&nbsp;
second strategy, we have statistical arbitrage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.600
Similar to convertible arbitrage, statistical&nbsp;
arbitrage aims to leverage price discrepancies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:11.600
This time, the goal is to leverage the discrepancy&nbsp;
between the market and an individual stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.320
or industry. Unless a stock receives some&nbsp;
crazy news or has a killer earnings report,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.960
it follows the overall market. Now, a&nbsp;
stock may be more volatile than the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:27.680
but this can also be measured by something called&nbsp;
beta. For example, for every 1% the S&amp;P 500 rises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:32.400
you may be able to expect Tesla to rise 5%&nbsp;
barring any external factors. But what if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:38.560
the market is up 1% and Tesla is also only up&nbsp;
1%. In this scenario, it’s statistically likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:44.800
that Tesla rises another 4%, so it would be a&nbsp;
relatively safe bet to buy Tesla stock. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:50.720
if the market is only up 1% but Tesla is up 9%,&nbsp;
it’s statistically likely that Tesla will drop 4%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.560
and it would be a safe bet to short Tesla stock.&nbsp;
Statistical arbitrage looks to leverage such over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:01.520
and underperformances of the market to squeeze&nbsp;
out some profits. And finally, moving onto Izzy’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:06.480
third key strategy, we have fundamental long and&nbsp;
short pairing. This is really the bread and butter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:11.840
of all hedge funds. You go ahead and buy some&nbsp;
stocks and short some stocks at the same time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:16.000
in order to reduce your overall exposure to the&nbsp;
market. Some funds employ neutral strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.480
where they have an equal amount in long and short&nbsp;
positions while other funds utilize long biases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:24.800
which means that they have more in long positions.&nbsp;
There’s really nothing unique or special about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:29.840
this strategy, but being a hedge fund, Izzy often&nbsp;
finds himself leveraging this strategy as well.

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:33.440
TRADING PHILOSOPHIES:&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:37.600
In terms of trading philosophies, Izzy only&nbsp;
has a few simple guidelines, but these have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.640
proven extremely valuable for him. His first rule&nbsp;
for trading is maintaining severe risk control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:46.240
When you’re playing with tens of billions&nbsp;
of dollars, this should really be a given,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.000
but it’s not uncommon to hear about hedge&nbsp;
funds managers taking outsized bets and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:55.040
getting destroyed. So, clearly sticking to&nbsp;
risk guidelines is not as easy as it sounds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:59.120
Moving onto Izzy’s second rule, we have be&nbsp;
committed to non-directional strategies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:03.440
As we saw with all of Izzy’s trading strategies,&nbsp;
none of them depended on the market going up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:08.800
or down. In fact, the direction of the market&nbsp;
had no impact on Izzy’s performance, and this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:13.520
extremely important because the hardest part isn’t&nbsp;
making money in a bull market or a bear market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.520 --> 00:08:18.320
but making money in a sideways market.&nbsp;
And finally, moving onto Izzy’s last rule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:25.840
we have a Warren Buffet type rule which is&nbsp;
never forget to observe the first two rules.

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.200
MILLENNIUM MANAGEMENT TODAY:
Applying these strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:31.520
and philosophies over 30 years has allowed&nbsp;
Millennium Management to rocket from $35 million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:36.480
in 1989 to $57 billion in assets today making&nbsp;
them one of the largest hedge funds in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.480 --> 00:08:40.960
world. They currently operate a total of&nbsp;
200 different investment teams and employ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:45.920
2800 people. If you’re thinking about getting a&nbsp;
job here though, you better be extremely tough as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:51.120
employment and compensation is strictly dependent&nbsp;
on quarterly performance. Millennium Management&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.120 --> 00:08:54.720
along with the rest of Wall Street has received&nbsp;
massive criticism for these high pressure work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:58.960
environments, but despite how the media paints&nbsp;
them, people continue to flock towards these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:03.760
companies because the upside potential is&nbsp;
extraordinary. As for Izzy Englander today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:08.800
he’s spent a lot of his efforts on supporting the&nbsp;
Jewish community as much as possible. Since 2006,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.680
the Englander Foundation has donated a total of&nbsp;
$20 million, most of which has gone to Jewish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:20.080
schools and organizations. He hasn’t been shy in&nbsp;
splurging on himself either. In 2014, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.680
he bought a duplex in New York’s Park Avenue for&nbsp;
a whopping $71.3 million which is a record price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.640
for a Manhattan co-op. At the end of the day,&nbsp;
Izzy’s life matches the hedge fund stereotype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:35.360
pretty well. He’s made his fortune on leveraging&nbsp;
miniscule price discrepancies in the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.080
and despite making so much money, it’s not clear&nbsp;
what sort of legacy and impact Millenium really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.440
has on the world. You could argue that Izzy is&nbsp;
just really good at a zero sum game, but he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:50.080
not creating any real value. Regardless of these&nbsp;
critiques though, it seems like Izzy is enjoying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:55.040
his life just fine though, and in the end, that’s&nbsp;
all that really matters. Would you guys consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:59.360
becoming a hedge fund manager? Comment that&nbsp;
down below. Also, drop a like if you wish&nbsp;&nbsp;

